title: A full adder from half adders
mode: vhdl
chapter: Arithmetic
check: behaves
---- intro
There is another way to build the full adder: from two half adders, as you would by hand.

- the first adds `a` and `b`: sum `s1`, carry `c1`
- the second adds `cin` to `s1`: that sum is `s`, and its carry is `c2`
- if either of them carried, the full adder carries: `cout` is `c1` or `c2`

The starter declares the three signals for you.
---- task
Build the full adder from two half adders, using the signals `s1`, `c1` and `c2`.
---- start
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity full_adder2 is
  port(
    a    : in  std_logic;
    b    : in  std_logic;
    cin  : in  std_logic;
    s    : out std_logic;
    cout : out std_logic
  );
end full_adder2;

architecture rtl of full_adder2 is
    signal s1, c1, c2 : std_logic;
begin
    -- write it here
end rtl;
---- answer
library ieee;
use ieee.std_logic_1164.all;

entity full_adder2 is
  port(
    a    : in  std_logic;
    b    : in  std_logic;
    cin  : in  std_logic;
    s    : out std_logic;
    cout : out std_logic
  );
end full_adder2;

architecture rtl of full_adder2 is
    signal s1, c1, c2 : std_logic;
begin
    s1   <= a xor b;
    c1   <= a and b;
    s    <= s1 xor cin;
    c2   <= s1 and cin;
    cout <= c1 or c2;
end rtl;
---- hint1
Five lines: two for the first half adder, two for the second, one for `cout`.
---- hint2
`s1 <= a xor b;` `c1 <= a and b;` `s <= s1 xor cin;` `c2 <= s1 and cin;` `cout <= c1 or c2;`
---- solution
```
s1   <= a xor b;
c1   <= a and b;
s    <= s1 xor cin;
c2   <= s1 and cin;
cout <= c1 or c2;
```
It does exactly what the two-line full adder did, built a different way.
---- done
Two different circuits, one behaviour: the checker tried every input and could not tell them apart. Designers pick whichever is smaller or faster.
